1
00:00:00,045 --> 00:00:05,180
It's eighteen forty six, and a woman in
labour arrives at Vienna General Hospital.

2
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:08,080
If she gets a fever
after the birth and dies,

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,260
any doctor here will tell you why.

4
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:11,370
Bad air.

5
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:13,530
But here's what nobody can explain:

6
00:00:13,530 --> 00:00:16,540
why does it matter so much
which day she walks in?

7
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:18,940
The hospital has two maternity clinics,

8
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:21,820
and they take women in on alternate days.

9
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:25,180
Doctors and medical
students run the first one,

10
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:27,020
and midwives run the second.

11
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:30,500
That year, one mother in nine
dies in the first clinic.

12
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:33,270
In the second, it's one in thirty six.

13
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:38,220
And the women know it, so well that they
beg on their knees to be taken anywhere

14
00:00:38,284 --> 00:00:39,119
else.

15
00:00:39,159 --> 00:00:39,470
Please.

16
00:00:39,510 --> 00:00:41,180
Not the first clinic.

17
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:41,940
Same building.

18
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:42,980
Same city.

19
00:00:43,036 --> 00:00:44,330
Same air.

20
00:00:44,383 --> 00:00:46,090
So if you were the doctor here,

21
00:00:46,186 --> 00:00:47,770
what would you check first?

22
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:51,550
That question eats at a young
doctor called Ignaz Semmelweis.

23
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:55,080
He's twenty eight, and the new
assistant in the first clinic.

24
00:00:55,636 --> 00:00:56,841
Everything was in question.

25
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,240
Only the large number of deaths
was an unquestionable reality.

26
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:04,060
So Semmelweis starts testing his guesses,

27
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:05,180
one at a time.

28
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:06,360
Maybe it's the air.

29
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:09,370
Yet women who give birth in the street,

30
00:01:09,423 --> 00:01:11,130
on their way to the hospital,

31
00:01:11,187 --> 00:01:12,810
hardly ever catch the fever.

32
00:01:12,810 --> 00:01:14,510
Maybe it's how they give birth.

33
00:01:14,510 --> 00:01:16,980
The midwives' mothers lie on their side,

34
00:01:17,033 --> 00:01:19,460
so he has the women in
his clinic do the same.

35
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:20,500
Nothing changes.

36
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,860
Maybe it's fear.

37
00:01:21,904 --> 00:01:23,700
Whenever a woman is dying,

38
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:26,640
a priest comes through
the ward to her bed,

39
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:29,120
and a man walks ahead
of him ringing a bell.

40
00:01:29,376 --> 00:01:33,360
Even to me myself it had a
strange effect upon my nerves,

41
00:01:33,392 --> 00:01:36,240
when I heard the bell hurry past my door.

42
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:39,200
So he sends the priest the long way round,

43
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:40,640
but the mothers keep dying.

44
00:01:40,782 --> 00:01:42,720
By now, whatever you guessed first,

45
00:01:42,827 --> 00:01:44,380
he's probably tried it.

46
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:46,830
And every time, he was wrong.

47
00:01:47,086 --> 00:01:49,310
Then, in March eighteen forty seven,

48
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:51,840
his friend Jakob Kolletschka,

49
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:53,740
a professor of forensic medicine,

50
00:01:53,927 --> 00:01:57,250
is cut by a student's
knife during an autopsy.

51
00:01:57,270 --> 00:01:58,270
And he dies.

52
00:01:58,550 --> 00:02:02,930
Day and night I was haunted by the
image of Kolletschka's disease.

53
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:05,240
And when they examine his friend's body,

54
00:02:05,272 --> 00:02:07,080
they find something he can't shake.

55
00:02:07,267 --> 00:02:09,870
It's the same disease
that's killing the mothers.

56
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:13,390
Now think about who does the
autopsies in this hospital.

57
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:14,240
The doctors.

58
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:15,550
The students.

59
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:19,230
The same people who walk from the morgue
straight to the mothers in the first

60
00:02:19,299 --> 00:02:19,870
clinic.

61
00:02:19,904 --> 00:02:21,510
With the same bare hands.

62
00:02:21,510 --> 00:02:23,660
So what are those hands carrying?

63
00:02:23,796 --> 00:02:25,740
Semmelweis is about to find out.

64
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:28,380
And what happens next
will change medicine.

65
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:31,630
But first, he'll have to convince a
world that doesn't want to listen.
